---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/use-cases/deliverability";
const title = "Shopify SMS deliverability incident workflow";
const description = "Build a transport, policy, feedback and expiry record before retrying a missing Shopify text or escalating to support.";
const faqs = [{"question": "Does a sent status prove delivery?", "answer": "Use the supplier\u2019s definition and receipt evidence. Provider acceptance or handoff is not the same as a final delivery report, and delivery reporting does not prove reading or a resulting purchase."}, {"question": "Should we resend when a receipt is missing?", "answer": "Keep the attempt unresolved, inspect its status and ask the supplier about the observation window. Recheck eligibility, expiry and duplicate prevention before any new attempt."}, {"question": "Which app has the best deliverability?", "answer": "This page establishes no ranking. Compare evidence export, sender readiness, failure explanations, cancellation boundaries and support response using the same controlled fixtures."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={description} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <h1>Shopify SMS deliverability: work the incident before retrying</h1>
    <p>A customer reports a missing restock text just as inventory runs down. The next action depends on whether the message was excluded, deferred, queued, rejected or handed to a carrier without a final receipt. This playbook turns that report into an evidence packet, a safe queue decision and a controlled recovery. It contains no merchant performance results or deliverability ranking.</p>
    <h2>Open an incident with a latest useful delivery time</h2>
    <p>Assign an incident owner and record the campaign, sender, destination market, offer deadline and stock dependency. Pause expansion of the affected campaign while investigating a material unexplained failure pattern. Set the decision time for cancellation before the offer expires; avoid letting a delayed restock invitation become tomorrow’s unavailable-stock complaint.</p>
    <p>Separate permission from transport. A correctly suppressed number should remain suppressed. Preserve the channel status and capture evidence before anyone changes a segment or reimports a recipient. Use the <a href="/guides/consent">consent guide</a> for the permission record and the <a href="/guides/deliverability">deliverability guide</a> for state definitions. This use case owns the incident handoff, recovery rehearsal and closeout.</p>
    <h2>Construct one timeline per attempted message</h2>
    <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Checkpoint</th><th>Evidence to save</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Eligibility</td><td>Profile identity, channel permission, audience evaluation and exclusion reason</td><td>Preserve valid exclusions; repair incorrect source data before reconsidering eligibility</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Policy deferral</td><td>Quiet-hours or frequency decision, scheduled release and expiry</td><td>Wait only while the message remains useful and permitted</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Provider acceptance</td><td>Message ID, submission time, queue age and status history</td><td>Find the existing attempt before creating another</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Handoff or rejection</td><td>Sender readiness, raw error and provider definition of the status</td><td>Route a documented configuration error to its owner; escalate ambiguous rejection</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Feedback</td><td>Receipt source/time, last status update and missing-receipt interval</td><td>Keep unresolved outcomes separate from confirmed failures and delivery reports</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360039239172">Klaviyo’s failure guide</a> locates failure reasons in Recipient activity and describes blocked messages as potentially blocked by a carrier, Klaviyo or the recipient. A dashboard label alone therefore does not isolate the responsible party. Save the exact reason and ask support which evidence narrows it.</p>
    <p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005258568">Klaviyo’s analytics documentation</a> distinguishes skipped recipients from failed deliveries. For a transport vocabulary example, <a href="https://help.twilio.com/articles/223134347-What-are-the-Possible-SMS-and-MMS-Message-Statuses-and-What-do-They-Mean">Twilio describes message states and receipts</a>. These sources explain their own products; do not assume another Shopify app exports identical labels or that it uses Twilio.</p>
    <h2>Route the cause to the right owner</h2>
    <p>A policy deferment belongs with the operator who owns destination rules and campaign expiry. A sender registration or account configuration rejection belongs with the messaging administrator. A cluster of carrier-filtering reasons needs the supplier’s review of sender, content and linked destination. An invalid-number pattern needs acquisition-source investigation, not a new creative variant. An isolated missing receipt remains unresolved until the supplier’s observation window or further evidence supports a conclusion.</p>
    <p>Compare affected and unaffected cohorts by destination, sender, capture source, message variant and time interval. Keep denominators consistent: excluded recipients are not carrier attempts. Report failed attempts divided by attempted submissions and unresolved attempts divided by attempted submissions as separate measures. A delivery report is transport evidence, not evidence that a person read the text or that the campaign caused a sale.</p>
    <h2>Expiry and cancellation are separate controls</h2>
    <p>Ask where cancellation stops working: inside the app, inside its provider queue, or after carrier handoff. <a href="https://www.twilio.com/docs/messaging/services">Twilio’s Messaging Services documentation</a> explains that carriers can queue messages beyond a provider validity period. Treat that as a boundary to investigate, not a promise that every app exposes a downstream recall function.</p>
    <p>For stock-limited messages, record both the latest useful dispatch time and the risk of downstream delay. If the supplier cannot stop an accepted attempt, prevent new submissions and make the landing page accurately reflect current availability. Do not replace an unresolved SMS with another promotional channel unless that channel has its own permission and the duplicate-contact policy allows it.</p>
    <h2>Proposed acceptance fixtures before the next launch</h2>
    <p>Use controlled recipients and supplier-approved test facilities. The following are proposed checks, not observed app results. Record the expected state before running each, then save the actual trace and an owner for any gap.</p>
    <table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Fixture</th><th>Expected control</th><th>Evidence required</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Email-only subscriber enters the restock audience</td><td>No promotional SMS attempt</td><td>Channel status, exclusion reason and absence of provider submission</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Quiet-hours deferral extends past the restock deadline</td><td>Cancel or expire the obsolete offer under the configured policy</td><td>Scheduled release, expiry and final queue decision</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Customer purchases while a reminder waits</td><td>Re-evaluate eligibility and stop the obsolete reminder</td><td>Order ingestion time and pending-message cancellation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Permission withdrawn before dispatch</td><td>No new promotional submission after the withdrawal is applied</td><td>Withdrawal receipt, processing time and dispatch trace</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Duplicate event or repeated status callback</td><td>No second send or double-counted final outcome</td><td>Event key, message ID and deduplication log</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Missing final receipt</td><td>Remain unresolved without automatic resend</td><td>Last provider state, observation window and escalation decision</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rejection with an ambiguous reason</td><td>Preserve raw feedback and route to support</td><td>Error export and support interpretation linked to the attempt</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <h2>Prepare a support packet that can be investigated</h2>
    <p>Include account and campaign identifiers, a small representative set of provider message IDs, UTC submission and update times, destination countries, sender type, exact error values and the status history. Add the message body and linked destination as sent, relevant sender approval state, and the expected versus actual result. Supply personal data only through the supplier’s approved private support channel, with recipient details limited to what investigation needs.</p>
    <p>Ask concrete questions: was the message submitted to a carrier; what produced the reason; can the attempt still deliver; can it be cancelled; what is the safe retry condition; and does the reported problem affect billing? Record the response against each message ID. An answer about general best practices does not resolve a specific unknown attempt.</p>
    <h2>Recover without multiplying the incident</h2>
    <p>Retry only after identifying the prior attempt’s state, correcting the cause, rechecking permission and confirming the offer is still valid. Assign a retry key to prevent multiple operators from resubmitting the same job. If the outcome remains unknown, escalate rather than treating silence as failure. A new platform or sender is not a substitute for diagnosing a content, permission or expiry problem.</p>
    <p>After a controlled recovery, reconcile attempted, excluded, deferred, failed, delivered-reported and unresolved records using the supplier’s definitions. Keep billing units separate from recipient counts: request the actual encoding, segmentation, destination and fee treatment for the message sent. Add investigation and support labor to the incident cost; there is no claimed recovery rate or revenue lift here.</p>
    <h2>Close the incident with an owner and a reusable check</h2>
    <p>Save the cause supported by evidence, remaining unknowns, affected interval, corrective configuration and the fixture that now passes. Assign ongoing review of raw failure reasons, aged queues and missing feedback. If app selection is still open, ask each candidate to demonstrate this same export, cancellation boundary and support process. That creates a relevant comparison without inventing a best-deliverability app.</p>
    <p>Next, apply <a href="/guides/quiet-hours">dispatch policy checks</a> and the <a href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel ownership worksheet</a>. The <a href="/compare/activecampaign-vs-klaviyo">ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo comparison</a> tests who owns account and commerce decisions before transport begins.</p>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions before implementation" />
</BaseLayout>
